Track 2: King of the Crosswalk
- Story & Context: Sarah, the group’s reluctant frontwoman, sat on the hood of a stalled taxi, her fingers tracing the keys of a portable interface that mirrored her stage-grand performance. She felt the heavy gaze of the precinct officers at the end of the block, but she only adjusted her high-vis collar and looked at the wet, oil-stained pavement. "I traded my rhythm for a riot just to hear the city scream," she confessed, her voice thick with the exhaustion of someone who has stopped fearing the sirens.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A solitary figure sitting on a rusted vehicle under a flickering streetlamp, deep blue and orange color palette, cinematic film grain, focus on the worn textures of a piano interface, moody and intimate.

Track 3: The Tollgate Requiem
- Story & Context: Years after the circuits were permanently dismantled, the legend of the Tollgate Requiem persists among the city's youth who gather in the abandoned underpass to leave offerings of copper wire. It is said that if the wind hits the ventilation shafts just right, the faint, resonant hammer-strike of a ghost piano plays the final, unresolved chord of that last illegal performance. "The rhythm is buried, but the wire still remembers the weight," the locals whisper, marking the end of the era where the city was an instrument.
